# Review — Tier 3, 3.1 Sum of digits  (2026-06-11)

## 1. Verdict
Works correctly for valid positive integers and for 0, but silently mishandles
negative input (wrong answer instead of rejection), and has two format-specifier
mismatches that -Wall will flag.

## 2. Compilation check
Compiles, but NOT clean under -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic. Two -Wformat warnings:
- Line 16 — sscanf "%d" with &n: %d expects int*, &n is unsigned int*.
- Line 27 — printf "%d" with sum: %d expects int, sum is unsigned int.
Fix with %u, or (better) switch the variables to signed int.

## 3. Correctness
Digit-extraction loop is correct: n % 10 peels the lowest digit, n / 10 shifts
right one decimal place.
- Normal (123) → 6. Correct.
- Zero (0) → loop skipped, prints 0. Correct.
- Negative (-5) → BUG. %d reads -5, stored into unsigned int as 4294967291;
  sscanf returns 1 so validation passes; prints "Sum of digits = 53". Confident
  wrong answer for input the prompt called invalid.
- Largest (4294967295) → digit sum 57, fits. No overflow.
- Garbage (abc) → sscanf returns 0, prints "Not an integer". Correct.

## 4. Concept gaps
1. Conversion-specifier <-> type matching (%d vs %u).
2. Signed->unsigned conversion / wrap-around (NOTE-CAST territory).
3. Domain validation: "parsed as a number" != "valid for this problem".

## 5. Mini-teaching
Specifiers + signedness: the bits of -5 don't change when stored in unsigned int;
only the interpretation changes. %d = interpret as signed, %u = as unsigned. The
loop reads unsigned, so it sees ~4.29 billion. The mismatch is silent because
signed/unsigned share a representation; -Wformat is the only safety net.

Validation: a clean parse only proves the text was numeric. Two separate checks:
  if (sscanf(line, "%d", &n) != 1) { /* not a number */ }
  if (n < 0)                        { /* number, but out of domain */ }

## 6. Alternative approach
Use signed int and validate the sign — kills both warnings and the bug at once:

    #include <stdio.h>
    int main(void) {
        char line[64];
        int n, sum = 0;
        printf("Enter a positive integer: ");
        if (fgets(line, sizeof line, stdin) == NULL) return 1;
        if (sscanf(line, "%d", &n) != 1) { printf("Not an integer\n"); return 1; }
        if (n < 0) { printf("Please enter a non-negative integer\n"); return 1; }
        while (n > 0) { sum += n % 10; n /= 10; }
        printf("Sum of digits = %d\n", sum);
        return 0;
    }

Types match the specifiers, negatives rejected explicitly, non-zero exit on error.
Portability note: you included <stdint.h> but used no fixed-width type — drop it.
Reach for uint32_t (+ PRIu32/SCNu32 from <inttypes.h>) only when a guaranteed
width genuinely matters on the MCU.

## 7. Style and good-practice notes
- Line 7: `x` is unclear — `digit` says what it is.
- Lines 13, 18: `return 0` on error means success to the OS — use `return 1`.
- Line 3: unused <stdint.h>.
- Line 6: stray space in `char line [64]` — conventional form is `line[64]`.